    slug: "module2",
    title: "Module 2",
    markdown: "# Module 2\n\n## Focus\n\nInstructional Design and Student Engagement \n\n### Instructional Design\n\nModule 2 helped me think more carefully about the connection between technology, instructional design, and student engagement. Technology can make lessons more interactive and visually engaging, but it should not be added only for novelty. A tool is most useful when it supports a clear learning objective, helps students practice or demonstrate understanding, and gives the teacher a better way to organize instruction or provide feedback.  \n\n### Student Engagement\n\nEngagement is not the same as entertainment. Students may enjoy using digital tools and playing games, but the goal should be meaningful participation in learning. Technology can support engagement by giving students multiple ways to interact with content, respond to questions, create products, collaborate with classmates, and reflect on their thinking. In mathematics and STEM instruction, this is especially valuable because digital tools can make abstract ideas more visual and easier to explore. \n\n### Standards and Purpose\n\nThe work in Module 2 also connected to larger technology standards and frameworks, including ISTE, AECT, and P21. These are listed in the References section for review. These frameworks helped me think about technology as part of a broader instructional purpose. Effective technology integration should support creativity, collaboration, communication, critical thinking, and responsible use. As this Digital Toolbox continues to add content, each tool is included because it has a clear classroom purpose and not simply because it is popular or easy to access.  \n\n### Digital Toolbox Growth\n\nThe PowerPoint project and Bitmoji activity showed different ways technology can be used to organize instruction and increase student interaction. These activities reminded me that technology can support both teacher productivity and student learning when it is designed around the needs of the classroom. \n\n## Classroom Connection\n\n### Tool-Supported Lesson Idea\n\nI could create a short activity where students work through slope, graphing, or equation questions and use linked slides or clickable objects to check their progress. This would allow students to practice important math skills in a more visu**al and interactive format while still staying connected to the learning objective. It would be a great use of the classroom Smartboards! Students like to interact with the board but I rarely see educational interactions.",
    focus: "Instructional Design and Student Engagement \n\n### Instructional Design\n\nModule 2 helped me think more carefully about the connection between technology, instructional design, and student engagement. Technology can make lessons more interactive and visually engaging, but it should not be added only for novelty. A tool is most useful when it supports a clear learning objective, helps students practice or demonstrate understanding, and gives the teacher a better way to organize instruction or provide feedback.  \n\n### Student Engagement\n\nEngagement is not the same as entertainment. Students may enjoy using digital tools and playing games, but the goal should be meaningful participation in learning. Technology can support engagement by giving students multiple ways to interact with content, respond to questions, create products, collaborate with classmates, and reflect on their thinking. In mathematics and STEM instruction, this is especially valuable because digital tools can make abstract ideas more visual and easier to explore. \n\n### Standards and Purpose\n\nThe work in Module 2 also connected to larger technology standards and frameworks, including ISTE, AECT, and P21. These are listed in the References section for review. These frameworks helped me think about technology as part of a broader instructional purpose. Effective technology integration should support creativity, collaboration, communication, critical thinking, and responsible use. As this Digital Toolbox continues to add content, each tool is included because it has a clear classroom purpose and not simply because it is popular or easy to access.  \n\n### Digital Toolbox Growth\n\nThe PowerPoint project and Bitmoji activity showed different ways technology can be used to organize instruction and increase student interaction. These activities reminded me that technology can support both teacher productivity and student learning when it is designed around the needs of the classroom.",
