    reflection: "### AI as a Teacher Planning Tool\n\nModule 3 helped me think more carefully about the role of artificial intelligence in education. I see AI being useful for teachers as both a planning tool and a content-generation tool. A teacher can use AI to brainstorm lesson ideas, create examples, organize instructional materials, generate practice questions, or think through different ways to explain a concept. Used appropriately, AI can support teacher productivity and help teachers prepare more varied learning experiences for students. \n\n### AI as Student Support\n\nI also think students should have access to AI as a tool for their own learning. For example, if I am teaching a math topic, I do not see a problem with a student using AI to ask questions, explore examples, or get another explanation of a concept. AI can function like a study support when students use it to learn in an appropriate way. At the same time, teachers need to design assignments and assessments so students still have to demonstrate their own understanding.\n\n### Boundaries and Assessment\n\nI do not think students should be restricted from AI use in every situation, but there are times when AI should not be used. Math assessment testing is one example where students need to show what they can do independently. Students should be able to stand on their own, and that requires practice, not just reading AI-generated content. A major concern is using AI during testing. Students should be honest about whether they actually knew the content or needed AI to reach the answer. \n\n### Creativity and Technology\n\nThis module also made me think about creativity and student expression. AI can support art, visual design, graphs, charts, and other creative products when it is used within the context of the student's own projects. I think that kind of creativity should be encouraged. The important distinction is whether AI is helping students develop, represent, or explain their ideas, or whether it is replacing the student's thinking entirely.",
    classroomConnection: "### AI-Supported Math Learning\n\nOne classroom connection from Module 3 is the idea of using AI as a support tool during math instruction. In a lesson on a Math topic, students could use AI to ask for a second explanation, generate a real-world example, or compare different ways to visualize the problem. The teacher could then ask students to explain the idea in their own words, solve a related problem without AI, or create an example that shows their understanding.\n\n### Responsible Use Routine\n\nA technology routine I would want to strengthen is helping students identify when AI is being used for learning and whether a student is on a path of understanding the content. Students could be asked to answer a simple reflection question such as, \"Did this tool help me understand the content, or did it give me an answer I could not explain?\" This would help connect AI use to honesty, responsibility, and actual learning.",
