    slug: "module6",
    title: "Module 6",
    markdown: "# Module 6\n\n## Date Range\n\nJuly 23–31, 2026\n\n## Focus\n\nTechnology Integration, Transformation of Learning, and Professional Growth\n\n## Reflection\n\n### Looking Back at the Course\n\nModule 6 gave me a chance to look back at the whole EDUC 675 course and think about how my understanding of educational technology has changed. At the beginning of the course, the Digital Toolbox felt more like a collection of separate assignments and tools. By the end, it started to feel more like an organized professional resource that I could actually return to when planning instruction.\n\nThe course introduced a lot of tools and tasks in a short period of time, and at first that can feel like a lot to manage. Repeating the process each module made it more manageable. I started to see the pattern: identify the instructional purpose, explore the tool, think about classroom use, and then reflect on whether the technology actually supports learning.\n\n### Transformation of Learning\n\nThe idea of transformation of learning helped me think beyond just learning how to operate new applications. Meaningful technology integration is not only about knowing which buttons to click. It involves reconsidering how lessons are designed, how students access information, how they practice, how they demonstrate understanding, and how teachers provide feedback.\n\nTechnology can transform learning when it creates useful options that were not as easy in a traditional lesson. A student might review a video more than once, use a visual model to explore a math idea, respond through a digital check-in, or collaborate through a structured activity. The important part is that the technology changes the learning experience in a purposeful way instead of just making the same worksheet appear on a screen.\n\n### Technology with Purpose\n\nOne theme that kept coming up for me is that technology should have a clear purpose. A tool should support the learning objective, organize the activity, improve access, give students a way to practice or create, help communication, or provide useful feedback. If it does not do one of those things, then it may not be worth using for that lesson.\n\nThis is especially important in math and STEM instruction. Students still need to reason, calculate, explain, revise, and check their own thinking. A digital tool can make the task more visual, interactive, or organized, but it should not remove the student's responsibility for learning.\n\n### Growth Across the Course\n\nAcross the course, my thinking developed through several different types of assignments. The early reflection on personal technology habits helped me think about intentional use. The instructional design and engagement work reminded me that engagement is not the same as entertainment. The AI and mind-mapping module helped me think about creativity, responsible use, and the need for students to still explain their own understanding.\n\nThe digital practice and escape-room work helped me see how technology can organize repetition, pacing, and feedback while students continue doing the math. The flipped classroom module added another layer because it required a full lesson sequence with video capture, lesson housing, guided notes, readiness checks, group work, and student feedback. These examples helped me move from thinking about tools individually to thinking about how tools fit into instruction.\n\n### Growth of the Digital Toolbox\n\nThe Digital Toolbox is more than a final course submission. It can continue to work as a planning reference, a record of tools I have explored, and a place to connect technology to classroom purpose. I do not expect to use every tool in every class, but having the tools organized makes it easier to choose something appropriate when a lesson needs it.\n\nI also like that the toolbox can keep growing. As I test tools in real classroom situations, I can return to the resource and update what worked, what did not work, and how each tool might fit a math, STEM, or CTE classroom.\n\n### Teacher and Learner Roles\n\nThis course also helped me think about how technology changes both the teacher's role and the student's role. The teacher has to plan more carefully, choose tools with purpose, give clear expectations, and provide alternatives when needed. The tool does not make those decisions for the teacher.\n\nStudents also have responsibility. They may have more ways to access information, participate, create, communicate, or reflect, but they still have to engage with the content. Whether students are using AI, a video lesson, a virtual escape room, or a practice tool, they need to be able to explain what they understand.\n\n## Classroom Connection\n\n### Future Technology Integration\n\nIn a future mathematics, STEM, or CTE classroom, I would start with the learning objective before choosing a tool. I would ask what students need to understand, what they need to do, and where students usually get stuck. Then I would decide whether technology adds a real benefit.\n\nIf a tool helps students see the concept more clearly, practice more effectively, receive feedback, collaborate, or show understanding in a better way, then it may be worth using. If the tool only adds novelty or makes the lesson more complicated, I would rather keep the lesson simpler. I would also want clear directions and an alternate path for students who need support or have access issues.\n\n### Closing Reflection\n\nAt the end of EDUC 675, I have a broader and more practical understanding of educational technology. The biggest growth for me is not simply knowing more tool names. It is knowing that technology has to be chosen and applied intentionally.\n\nThe Digital Toolbox will remain useful beyond this course because it gives me a place to return to when I am planning lessons and trying to decide what kind of support students need. Moving forward, I want my technology use to stay connected to instructional purpose. The question should always be whether the tool helps students learn, practice, explain, create, or reflect in a meaningful way.",
    focus: "Technology Integration, Transformation of Learning, and Professional Growth",
